00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.220
ALLAN NALM: Yeah, thanks, Brett.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.700
So back in the early
2000s, Google was off

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.070
indexing the whole
world wide web,

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.690
and its goal is
to build a service

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.610
at planetary scale, where
anyone around the world

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.790
basically can get a response
back in milliseconds, right?

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.370
But when you go back
12, 14 years ago,

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.090
Google was like any
company you see today.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:28.690
Applications were built running
on top of infrastructure,

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.700
and there was this dependency on
the underlying infrastructure.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.020
And we knew we needed to scale
and really scale quickly.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.800
So we went off
and tried to solve

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.550
two very discrete problems.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.840
Number one, how do
you take a workload

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.660
and deploy it on a machine
and do it predictably,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:50.360
and then deploy a variety of
workloads on the same machine

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.240
so you get better efficiency?

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.550
And then how do you
take this machine

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.370
and attach it to
a pool of compute.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.620
So as developers,
you just write code,

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.350
and you don't really care
where that application ends

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.880
up running, right?

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.930
That's what we went
off to solve back then.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.940
And now is really the
advent of containers.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.710
Google has been using
containers for many years--

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:13.600
over a decade.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.090
Back in-- about 14 years ago, we
open sourced a lot of the work

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.750
around cgroups.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.460
That really helped
running containers

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.720
in isolation and so forth.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.300
So as you look at where Google
is today, everything at Google

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.060
actually runs inside a
container, all our services,

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.140
from Ads to Search.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.890
A matter of fact,
for Google Cloud,

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.450
our virtual machines
are actually

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.130
running inside a container.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:43.990
And we spin up over four
billion containers per week.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.050
A lot of the work that we did
around containers really led

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:51.460
to the creation of an internal
system Google uses called Borg.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.670
All of our developers
that write applications

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.650
submit their jobs to Borg.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.530
And then Borg schedules
all these workloads

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.000
across Google's fleet.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.290
A few years back, with
the advent of containers

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.330
and the popularity
of containers,

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.810
Google decided to take all
the various best practices

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.360
associated with running
and operating and managing

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.140
containers internally with Borg
and create this open source

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.180
project called Kubernetes.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.610
And really, that was the
genesis behind Kubernetes

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.440
that we announced
around four years ago.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:25.510
And we open sourced it.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.610
And the goal of
Kubernetes is really

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:31.670
to manage and operate around
your container workloads,

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:32.170
right?

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.840
It was really inspired
by the best practices

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.420
that Google had around running,
managing, and operating

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.460
containers internally.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.500
And the design premise
behind Kubernetes

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.350
is we wanted it to
run anywhere, right?

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.590
And that was really the goal
of basically open sourcing it.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.020
And then working with
the community in terms

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.030
of providing support,
whether you're running it

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:56.650
on Google Cloud or running it
on any cloud, on bare metal,

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:59.470
on any virtualization
technology,

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:00.910
the whole goal
with Kubernetes is

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.430
just to make it
easy, open, flexible,

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:07.930
and basically the ability
to run it anywhere you want.

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:09.320
It was written in Go.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:13.170
And as I mentioned, from day
one we open sourced this.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.285
And the popularity of
Kubernetes right now

00:04:15.285 --> 00:04:17.920
is it's become the
de facto standard

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.589
for running and managing
containers out in the market.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:22.630
Really, the success is
behind the community

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:28.540
behind it and the API that it
brought forward to the world.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.274
When you're getting
started with Kubernetes--

00:04:31.274 --> 00:04:33.190
just a show of hands,
how many people attended

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:35.270
the keynote this morning?

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:35.770
Great.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:41.260
So you've probably heard about
the announcements around GKE

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:46.510
and bringing the GKE experience
to on-prem and things

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:47.740
of that nature.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.980
Before this announcement,
getting Kubernetes up

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:53.890
and running outside
of Google Cloud

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:58.650
really involved using a mix
and match of different tooling.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.270
It was an inconsistent
experience,

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:02.812
depending on where
you're running it.

00:05:02.812 --> 00:05:03.894
There are different tools.

00:05:03.894 --> 00:05:05.580
And we went off and
tried to figure out,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.690
how do we make this consistent?

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:10.510
But at Google, we have
this notion called

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.970
separation of concerns, right?

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:16.060
And although DevOps is a
very important function,

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:19.030
oftentimes when you give a
developer a double-edged sword,

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.400
where on one hand you're
asking them to write code,

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.670
and on the other hand
you're asking them to worry

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:28.270
about debugging,
troubleshooting, deployments,

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:30.170
it's not a very
optimal thing to do.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:32.470
And at Google we have
different functions.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:34.480
We have our
developers, and then we

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:39.490
have this specialization called
an application ops person.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.170
And then we have
another specialization

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.210
called the cluster ops person.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.910
And in the case of Kubernetes,
a cluster ops person

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:48.280
would come along and
create the environment.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:50.230
That's your day zero, right?

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:52.390
And once you have this
environment up and running

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.180
and you're provisioned
on top of nodes,

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.980
and you've configured
networking,

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.500
and you've set up all
the various agents

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.070
and containerized
services in the cluster,

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.620
it's not a simple process.

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:07.120
And the cluster
administrator really

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.750
is focused on getting all
of that up and running.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:14.350
From there, you basically
have now this API

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:17.710
that you can use to deploy
your containerized applications

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.520
in a consistent fashion.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.450
And once that's available,
now the application ops person

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.990
can come along and deploy their
containerized applications

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:29.770
in such a way where it's
resilient, it's scalable,

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:30.950
and so forth.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.550
So the role of
the developer ends

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.520
where the role of the
application ops person starts.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.890
And the role of the
application person

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:42.130
stops where the role of
the cluster admin starts.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:46.460
Now, you might be wondering, how
do I do all this in the cloud?

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:49.450
Well, at Google, we
introduced, three years ago,

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:52.971
a managed Kubernetes service
called Google Kubernetes

00:06:52.971 --> 00:06:53.470
Engine.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:56.240
It's been GA now
for three years.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.900
It's very much
managed Kubernetes.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.484
It's an API call to
create a cluster.

00:07:01.484 --> 00:07:03.400
We're actually going to
show you demo of this.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.470
Our demo's going to
go through everything

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:08.060
that we're talking about, and
we're going to do it live.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.210
But it's really an API
call to create a cluster.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.250
You specify how
many nodes you need.

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:16.270
You specify the region you want
your nodes, which zones you

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.670
want them deployed in.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.250
You can specify a whole
bunch of other parameters

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:22.377
that we'll go through.

00:07:22.377 --> 00:07:22.960
But that's it.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.450
After a few minutes,
you have a cluster now.

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:29.170
And you have an API that you can
use to actually run and manage

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.210
your containerized applications.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.700
On top of that,
Kubernetes Engine

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.130
provides you a
level of automation

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.950
around node auto-repair, right?

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:40.990
So your worker
nodes, where you're

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.810
running your applications,
every three months,

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.940
there's a new version
of Kubernetes.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:47.950
And we basically manage--

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:50.560
give you the option
of specifying

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.230
if you want your nodes
auto-upgraded, if you

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:54.959
want your master auto-upgraded.

00:07:54.959 --> 00:07:57.250
If there's an issue with one
of your nodes, we'll go in

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:59.840
and we'll repair the node.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.770
There's integrated auto-scaling
with the underlying virtual

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:03.700
machines.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.100
So you can configure
everything from your container

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:09.160
auto-scaling all the way down
to your node auto-scaling.

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:11.780
And again, we'll show you how
all that works in our demo.

00:08:13.802 --> 00:08:16.010
BRETT HESTERBERG: With that,
let me ask the audience,

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:21.600
how many of you have experience
with Google Cloud SQL?

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.237
OK, about a third or so.

00:08:23.237 --> 00:08:24.570
I'll give a little introduction.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.944
And of course, you'll see this
as we begin to demonstrate

00:08:26.944 --> 00:08:29.010
our application deployment.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:31.950
Let me start by saying where
Cloud SQL fits in Google's

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:33.830
overall database portfolio.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:35.549
The database portfolio
in Google Cloud

00:08:35.549 --> 00:08:37.289
is large and ever-growing.

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:40.260
You see rightmost on the slide
a number of database offerings

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:41.159
from our partners.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:43.740
Google Cloud has a
large partner ecosystem.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:47.640
And we list here a handful
of database partners

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.320
who have offerings
on Google Cloud.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.630
This is not an exhaustive list.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:54.870
Leftmost on the slide you
see our in-memory database.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.720
We pretty recently launched
a managed Redis service.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.490
So if you have caching
needs, take a look

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:02.090
at Cloud Memorystore which
recently launched Redis

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:03.420
into beta.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.290
Just right of center is our
relational portfolio comprised

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:09.330
of Cloud Spanner and Cloud SQL.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.496
If you haven't taken a
look at Cloud Spanner,

00:09:11.496 --> 00:09:12.870
there are a number
of talks going

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.210
on this week at the
conference, and I encourage you

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:16.780
to get to know that product.

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:19.620
Cloud SQL in the
relational portfolio

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:23.040
has one core commitment,
and that is compatibility.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.410
We offer as close to unmodified
MySQL and Postgres as we can,

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:32.490
so that if your application
works with MySQL or Postgres,

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.039
it just works with Cloud SQL.

00:09:34.039 --> 00:09:35.580
In today's demonstration,
we're going

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:37.430
to be using Cloud
SQL for Postgres.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:39.780
But the compatibility
story is essentially

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.580
Cloud SQL's core promise.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:44.580
In addition to
compatibility, Cloud SQL

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:47.940
aims to reduce mundane
database administration

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:54.120
tasks commonly associated with
running MySQL and Postgres.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.840
When we think about the Cloud
SQL stack and what it can do,

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.570
I think Tim Kelton
from Descartes Labs

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.370
does a good job
of summing this up

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:04.920
in terms of how he
sees SQL for his team.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.950
He notes here that his team's
using Cloud SQL at Descartes

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.450
Labs because it
allows them to focus

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.040
their time on other things,
things that more valuable

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.220
to them and to their customers.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.800
When I tend to think
of the Cloud SQL stack,

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.950
I picture this diagram,
where this, in my mind,

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:22.710
is what it takes to run
a database yourself.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:25.740
You have leftmost on
the slide a column

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:28.290
that shows essentially the
hardware, racking and stacking

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:32.670
of servers, thinking about power
and cooling for those servers.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:35.279
As you move left, we get
to the operating system,

00:10:35.279 --> 00:10:37.570
where we're worried about
installation and maintaining,

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:39.360
updating that operating system.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.730
Continuing to move left, we
finally get to the database

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:42.240
itself.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:43.420
We're installing that database.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:44.461
We're keeping it patched.

00:10:44.461 --> 00:10:45.600
We're keeping it secure.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.550
We're setting up things
like regular backups

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:48.930
for data protection.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.180
And finally, rightmost
in the slide,

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:53.250
we get into some
advanced kind of things.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:56.130
We're talking about
here high availability,

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.030
setting up replication
for scale out,

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:01.320
making decisions about when
a database is unhealthy,

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.110
should we automatically
failover, failover manually?

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:07.410
And the breadth
of this stack, we

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:09.770
have to plumb this through
to some monitoring systems

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:12.220
so we can keep tabs
on our environment.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:16.320
The goal of Cloud SQL is to
turn this technology stack

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.410
into an API call,
so that if you're

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:20.400
a DevOps engineer,
a developer, you

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.880
can set up, with a single API
call, a very simple development

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:27.540
database or a much more advanced
production database that

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:31.020
includes automatic failover
over for high availability

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:33.300
and replication
for read scale out.

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:36.990
Again, the goal here is
simplify this picture, such

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.880
that this turns into an
API or a few UI clicks,

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:41.700
as you'll see in our demo.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.200
What isn't covered
on this slide,

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.420
and what's not shown
in this diagram,

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:47.130
is what backs Cloud SQL.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:50.670
And Jason from RealMassive
points this out.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.370
He notes that Cloud SQL, and
managed services generally

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:56.040
in Google Cloud, give him
and his team peace of mind

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:00.690
because they are backed by
dedicated 24-by-7 SRE teams.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:03.660
For Cloud SQL, this is the
team writing the automation,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.250
writing the monitoring
to make sure

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.284
that our fleet of
databases is healthy.

00:12:07.284 --> 00:12:08.700
And when needed,
they're the folks

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.640
who get on the keyboard to
bring an unhealthy database back

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.140
to life.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:16.920
Hopefully, that gives you just
a feel for what Cloud SQL is.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:20.670
Let's now talk a little
less and start our building.

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:22.920
We'll remind everybody that
what we're building today

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:25.560
is a fairly simple
web application.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:27.810
It's about 200 lines
of Python code.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:29.310
The language doesn't
matter so much.

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:32.280
We're taking a web application
that we'll test and then

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:36.600
finally deploy here in a way
that uses Kubernetes Engine

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:38.760
and Cloud SQL to make
sure it's both fault

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:40.980
tolerant and scalable.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:42.570
Again, the picture
here illustrates

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:45.425
that we're going to span our
application across two zones

00:12:45.425 --> 00:12:46.945
so that we're fairly
fault tolerant.

00:12:46.945 --> 00:12:48.320
And if all goes
well, we're going

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.970
to throw some faults at it.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:52.830
Before we get into
this, I want to note

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:54.690
two additional key
components we're using.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.856
We've talked a lot about
Kubernetes Engine and Cloud

00:12:56.856 --> 00:12:57.840
SQL.

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:00.300
We're going to be introducing
the notion of the Cloud SQL

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:01.260
proxy.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.720
And what you see in this
diagram is the proxy

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:07.020
is a client-side installable
that makes your Cloud SQL

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:08.860
database look local.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:11.190
So if you were to install
the proxy on your laptop,

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:14.700
and you fired it up at
your favorite coffee shop,

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:17.490
your Cloud SQL
database would appear

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:19.410
to be local on that laptop.

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:21.180
The Cloud SQL proxy
behind the scenes

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.165
is setting up a secure tunnel
to your database automatically.

00:13:24.165 --> 00:13:26.950
And to the application
that you're developing,

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.672
you can act like, or
make it believe, that you

00:13:29.672 --> 00:13:30.880
are running a local database.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:32.320
And you'll see that in the demo.

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:34.236
This is really important
for Kubernetes Engine

00:13:34.236 --> 00:13:37.320
because what we're going to be
starting up pods and bringing

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:38.310
them down rapidly.

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:39.600
And we want to make
sure that we don't

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:41.970
have to deal with things like
white-listing IP addresses

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:42.660
on a firewall.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.150
We let the proxy do all
that sort of authentication

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:46.800
and connection for us.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:50.190
The other key concept here
is Google Cloud Shell.

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:53.999
How many of you have
used Google Cloud Shell?

00:13:53.999 --> 00:13:54.540
That's great.

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:55.729
So about half the room.

00:13:55.729 --> 00:13:57.270
That's actually more
than we thought.

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:58.770
We joke that
sometimes Cloud Shell

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:01.280
is one of our best
kept secrets in GCP.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.170
Cloud Shell is a VM that you
get for free, provisioned to you

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:05.970
with your Google Cloud account.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.595
We're going to be using it today
as our development environment

00:14:08.595 --> 00:14:10.920
because it includes a lot of
useful tools for a variety

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:12.589
of languages, including Python.

00:14:12.589 --> 00:14:14.130
It also includes a
Docker environment

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.338
that we're going to use as
we start to build and test

00:14:16.338 --> 00:14:17.670
our application.

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:22.680
OK, with that, Allan, let's get
into the actual build itself.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:27.430
We're going to start with
a Cloud SQL instance.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:30.120
And so you see my project here.

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.760
And we can adjust the font
as needed so folks can see.

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:35.179
I'm going to start by
creating a Cloud SQL instance.

00:14:35.179 --> 00:14:36.720
And let me just zoom
in a little bit.

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:43.890
When you're creating
a Cloud SQL instance--

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.100
I'm going to choose
Postgres in this case--

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:48.630
we answer a few fairly
straightforward questions.

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:51.250
We'll say this is
our instance name.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.139
I knew I'd typo right away.

00:14:56.139 --> 00:14:57.680
All right, this is
our instance name.

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:00.960
I'm going to use a password
that I highly do not recommend.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.560
You can all see it here.

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:06.570
This is way short and very
prone to dictionary attack.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:08.280
Please don't use this.

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:10.860
We're going to deploy Cloud
SQL and Kubernetes Engine

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:12.180
in US central.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.790
Obviously, you can deploy
it in any region you'd like.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:19.000
And I'm going to pick Zone B,
as in Bravo, for our instance.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:21.210
Now let's ask Cloud
SQL to take care

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:22.710
of some administration for us.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.270
As a starting point, I'm
going to size Cloud SQL here.

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:28.740
Cloud SQL starts at very
small, inexpensive instances,

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:31.460
less than $10 a month, if
you haven't used Cloud SQL.

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:35.250
And it scales way up right
now to 64 core machines.

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:37.100
This number is ever increasing.

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:38.890
You'll see higher
numbers in the future.

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:42.960
But for today, we'll give Cloud
SQL, say, eight compute cores.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:44.550
And we'll max out
RAM, which is always

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:45.810
a good idea for our database.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:48.105
We expect some traffic
on our web application.

00:15:48.105 --> 00:15:49.480
The nice thing
about Cloud SQL is

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:51.060
you can change
these at any time.

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:54.680
We can scale up later or
scale back down if we need to.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:56.430
One thing that's a bit
less intuitive when

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.680
I'm thinking about scaling
Cloud SQL or starting it up

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:01.050
is scaling my
storage performance.

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:03.030
In Google Cloud
and in Cloud SQL,

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.610
performance to scale by
adding storage capacity.

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:08.224
And you see that Cloud
SQL includes a performance

00:16:08.224 --> 00:16:10.140
calculator here, which
right now is showing me

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:12.990
that we just have a sliver
of the performance we could

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:14.820
get for our instance here.

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:18.239
I can increase that performance
by increasing capacity.

00:16:18.239 --> 00:16:20.280
And I'll give it a little
more because, again, we

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:23.250
think our web app is
going to be a popular one.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:26.820
I mentioned that Cloud SQL aims
to help manage mundane database

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:28.160
administration tasks.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.050
And a good example is the
automatic storage increase.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:33.570
This is a simple checkbox
in the Create flow.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:38.250
What it does is tell Cloud SQL,
hey, keep an eye on my disk.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:40.530
Make sure I don't run
out of disk space.

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:42.600
Before I do, increase
it a little bit

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:45.480
and continue to do so that I
don't run out of disk space.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:46.890
This, I think, is
a great example

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:49.894
of what we're talking about when
we say managing the mundane.

00:16:49.894 --> 00:16:51.810
I'll leave this checked
so that, just in case,

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:54.780
we don't run out of disk
today during the demo.

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:57.360
As we look a bit more,
we'll ask Cloud SQL

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:58.969
to automate our backups.

00:16:58.969 --> 00:17:00.635
We'll have it do so
pretty late at night

00:17:00.635 --> 00:17:03.450
to keep it out of the way of our
production runs during the day.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:06.270
And as a key point, I'm going
to tell Cloud SQL that we

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:07.890
want to be highly available.

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:10.025
When I do this, Cloud
SQL gets the message

00:17:10.025 --> 00:17:11.400
that it has some
extra work to do

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:14.369
to make sure that this instance
is a bit more fault tolerant.

00:17:14.369 --> 00:17:16.470
OK, with those
options selected, I'm

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:18.390
not going to bother
authorizing any networks.

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:21.270
We're going to let the Cloud
SQL handle our connection

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:22.500
security for us.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.950
And I won't bother setting
up any more configuration.

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:27.069
We'll just go ahead and
create our instance.

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:30.460
A Cloud SQL instance takes
about three minutes to create.

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:32.250
So fortunately for
all of you, I have,

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.440
very Martha Stewart style,
one fresh from the oven

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.210
ready for us to use.

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:39.510
And with that-- thank you, by
the way, for the few laughs.

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:42.270
With that, I'm going to fire
up Cloud Shell, as I mentioned,

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:44.410
one of, potentially,
our best kept secrets.

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:47.990
And let's get to
developing our application.

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:49.470
As Cloud Shell
comes up, remember,

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:50.910
this is the VM
that's provisioned

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:52.510
for you in your project.

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:54.420
It comes with a number
of tools built in.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:56.400
I've done just a little
bit of setup work.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:00.780
What I did was install
the Cloud SQL proxy

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:03.540
and set up a service account
so that I can authenticate

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:07.540
or connect to any Cloud
SQL instance in my project.

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:10.074
So let me go ahead and
start up the proxy.

00:18:10.074 --> 00:18:12.240
And then I'll show you what
a connection looks like.

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:19.290
OK, so we'll start up the proxy.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:20.200
This looks good.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:23.190
And then I'm going to go ahead
and connect using Cloud Shell's

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.380
built-in Posgres client.

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:27.060
And what you'll
notice here as I type

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:30.790
this command is that I'm
going to tell my Cloud Shell

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:33.480
VM that the database
is running locally.

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:36.510
You see that I'm using
localhost as the address.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:40.020
When I do that, the Cloud
SQL proxy intercepts,

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:41.790
and it asks for
my password, which

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:45.330
I'll put in my very
secure password here.

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:46.500
And we've connected here.

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:49.787
Let me make sure that we
have an empty database.

00:18:49.787 --> 00:18:51.870
We just created this
database, so let's make sure.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:53.280
Let's ask Postgres
if it's empty.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.960
Whoops.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:57.551
OK, Postgres says
I've got nothing

00:18:57.551 --> 00:18:59.800
in my tables, no tables at
all, which is a good thing.

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:01.508
We've got a brand new
database, and we're

00:19:01.508 --> 00:19:03.340
ready to use it for
our application.

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:06.100
What you've seen here is that
I've got good connectivity now

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:08.470
from my Cloud Shell
VM to my database.

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:10.374
So with that, let's
get our app started.

00:19:10.374 --> 00:19:11.790
One of the things
to note-- you're

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:14.340
more than welcome to take
pictures as we go along.

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:17.280
Everything Allan and I do today
is captured in a public code

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.680
lab, so you can go
through this yourself

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:21.810
when you're ready
on your own PC.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:23.880
What I will show you
is the application

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.340
that we've pre-built for
you as part of the code lab.

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:29.520
As I mentioned, it's a Python
application, a pretty small web

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:30.180
app.

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:32.700
And I'll download it
now to our Cloud Shell.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.440
This is hosted
right now on GitHub.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:39.270
And again, it's available for
you as part of the code lab.

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:41.010
We're writing this
application in Python,

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:43.020
but the language itself
doesn't matter as much.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:44.940
For those who are
familiar with Python,

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:47.230
I'm going to go ahead and set up
a virtual environment so we can

00:19:47.230 --> 00:19:48.396
get our development started.

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:55.340
And we will also then go ahead
and activate the environment

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:59.250
before we can get some
necessary modules downloaded,

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:01.610
some dependencies
for our application.

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:03.980
So I've got my
environment activated now.

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:07.010
Let me go get some dependencies.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:10.010
The pre-built app comes
with a requirements list

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:11.930
that you'll see as
one of the files.

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:16.100
And I can go and pip install
a bunch of dependency modules.

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:19.250
For those who are familiar
with Python and web frameworks,

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:21.590
you'll notice that most of
these dependencies are Flask.

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:24.650
We're running a
Flask web app today.

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:25.940
So we've downloaded these.

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:30.000
Let's go ahead and
test our application.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.430
So I'm going to
launch this directly

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:34.310
within the Cloud Shell VM.

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:35.960
And a really useful
feature here is

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:39.140
that Cloud Shell builds in a
local web host environment.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:45.717
So I can test my web application
right here from the Cloud VM.

00:20:45.717 --> 00:20:46.550
Let's bring this up.

00:20:46.550 --> 00:20:48.174
And what you see is
our app is at least

00:20:48.174 --> 00:20:50.340
deployed as a test application.

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:52.730
We're creating a Meme
Gen application, which

00:20:52.730 --> 00:20:54.320
is very important at Google.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:58.200
And I will go ahead and
create the first meme.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:02.390
Let me put in our meme here.

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:05.520
We'll see if our application can
happily talk to our database.

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:06.210
And there we go.

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:08.630
We've got our first
meme of the day.

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:09.777
Let me navigate back.

00:21:09.777 --> 00:21:11.360
We'll just see that
the app's working.

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:13.650
We should just have
our one meme in Recent.

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:16.190
OK, so we know now
our application

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:18.350
is working, at least
for test purposes.

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:21.830
Let's navigate back
to our Cloud Shell

00:21:21.830 --> 00:21:27.560
where I will go ahead and
kill our web host real quick

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:31.280
So now, if I look at our
other tab and I do a refresh,

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:33.450
we should see, yep,
our web app is down.

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:36.110
So we don't have our
test running anymore,

00:21:36.110 --> 00:21:37.200
which is great.

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:41.120
What we're going to do now
is start to use Docker.

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:43.120
We're going to containerize
our application now

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:45.120
that we've tested it and
know that it's running.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.840
What you see here is
that our Cloud Shell VM

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:49.790
has some Docker tools built in.

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:53.600
And I'm creating now a
container that we will go ahead

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:56.840
and deploy using Docker within
our Cloud Shell VM as a way

00:21:56.840 --> 00:22:00.440
to test again and get ready
for our deployment to GKE.

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:02.240
As this is building,
Allan, can you

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:04.160
talk a little bit
about the general steps

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:07.592
to take an application like
we have now and get it to GKE?

00:22:07.592 --> 00:22:09.050
ALLAN NALM: Yeah,
so the first step

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:11.590
is containerizing
the application.

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:13.100
Once it's containerized,
we're going

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:17.540
to push the image into
Google Container Registry.

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:21.170
And Google Container Registry
is the private repo for you

00:22:21.170 --> 00:22:24.590
to store your container images.

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:27.950
Keep in mind that you can use
Google Container Registry not

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:31.550
only for Google Kubernetes
Engine or Google Cloud,

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:35.070
but you can use it from
outside Google Cloud as well.

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:40.190
So once this image is stored
in Google Container Registry,

00:22:40.190 --> 00:22:44.600
we'll then be in a position
to specify and build

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:47.790
our Kubernetes
manifest to go pull

00:22:47.790 --> 00:22:49.940
from Container
Registry the images

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:52.759
that we want
deployed in our pods.

00:22:52.759 --> 00:22:54.050
BRETT HESTERBERG: That's great.

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:56.270
So it looks like our
container has built.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:22:58.880
I'm going to go ahead and run
it in Docker in our Cloud Shell

00:22:58.880 --> 00:22:59.000
VM.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:00.170
We've got that running.

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:03.050
And what this should mean
is that as I navigate back

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:07.940
to our test, or our local web
app, if I refresh this app,

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:09.890
we've now containerized
the application.

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:11.390
And ideally, this should work.

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:12.740
There's is our Meme Gen back.

00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:15.390
We stored our first meme
in the Cloud SQL database,

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:16.760
so it's been persisted.

