00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:07.310
So what we're doing
here is we're actually

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:09.690
creating a Kubernetes
cluster that spans

00:27:09.690 --> 00:27:12.040
multiple availability zones.

00:27:12.040 --> 00:27:15.360
So think of an availability
zone as a fault domain, right?

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:20.040
And the whole goal here is our
nodes will span availability

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:22.590
zones, so when we
deploy or applications,

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:27.990
the replicas of the application
will be across the region.

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:31.200
So this gives us higher
availability and resiliency

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:32.790
for our application.

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:36.700
And let's just walk through
some of the configurations

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:38.760
associated with the cluster.

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:41.440
When you go and create a
cluster, by default it's zonal.

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:43.740
So your control plane
and the worker nodes

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:45.510
will all be in the same zone.

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:48.000
But you have an option
to change it to regional.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:49.800
And when you change
it to regional,

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:52.800
you now have the ability for
your control plane, which

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:55.680
is your master, to be
replicated across three

00:27:55.680 --> 00:27:57.580
zones within the region.

00:27:57.580 --> 00:28:00.480
And then you specify
how many nodes you want

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:02.160
for this particular cluster.

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:04.170
And then those
nodes get deployed

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:09.180
across the various zones
within that particular region.

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:13.200
I mentioned earlier that
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:16.890
having been a GA product
for three years now,

00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:19.950
we feel like we have the most
sophisticated, most mature

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:22.110
managed Kubernetes
offering out there

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:24.330
on the market across any cloud.

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:26.585
Typically, when there's a
new version of Kubernetes

00:28:26.585 --> 00:28:29.220
out there released
every three months,

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:31.230
Google Kubernetes
Engine has support

00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:35.280
for that upstream version
within a couple of weeks.

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:38.070
So part of the process
is you basically

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:42.970
specify how many CPUs you
want for each machine type.

00:28:42.970 --> 00:28:46.260
By default, the node
operating system

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.520
is Container-Optimized OS.

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:50.730
This is a Chromium-based
operating system

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:55.470
that's very secure that Google
has created, Google maintains.

00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:58.980
If you want more flexibility
from a host operating system

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:02.420
standpoint, we also
provide support for Ubuntu.

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:05.760
Now, if you scroll
down a bit more,

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:08.640
you provide information
around how many

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:11.350
nodes you want in your
particular cluster.

00:29:11.350 --> 00:29:14.174
And that's really around
the day zero experience.

00:29:14.174 --> 00:29:16.590
Now, there's a whole bunch of
parameters really associated

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:18.600
with the day one experience.

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:20.610
So you can specify,
for example, I

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:22.740
want Google to
automatically upgrade

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:24.780
my nodes when there's
a new version,

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:26.730
or I want to be notified.

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:29.520
If something happens
to a particular node,

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:32.700
it fails, I'd like
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:36.300
to basically repair that
node and fix that node.

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:41.010
Same thing with patching,
all that is very--

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:44.200
we have an SRE team that
basically manages the services.

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:47.370
And if anything happens
to your cluster,

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:50.000
that SRE team will
fix the problem.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:53.040
There's built-in integration
with Stackdriver logging

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:54.240
and monitoring.

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:58.590
Scroll down, click on More.

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:00.490
You can label your cluster.

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:03.960
You can assign additional
zones that you want your nodes

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:06.040
to be distributed across.

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:09.420
There's auto-scaling not
only at the pod level,

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:10.780
but at the node level.

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:13.550
So you can integrate with the
underlying managed instance

00:30:13.550 --> 00:30:16.410
group framework for
Google Compute Engine

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:20.700
to initiate an out-of
scale from your pod level

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:22.980
all the way down to
your cluster level.

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:25.830
So we would add additional
nodes to your cluster

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:27.450
if there's resource contention.

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:34.560
You can configure persistent
disk storage to your cluster

00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:37.830
and basically map the
persistent disk resources,

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:40.240
depending on where your
pods are running, which

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:41.910
zone that they're running in.

00:30:41.910 --> 00:30:45.210
And then one of the really cool
features of Google Kubernetes

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:49.110
Engine is the integration
with Cloud networking.

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:52.410
The fact that Google Cloud
networking is a global service,

00:30:52.410 --> 00:30:56.640
you can assign a
VPC to your cluster.

00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:59.610
And that cluster
now has the ability

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:02.370
to take advantage
of a global network

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:04.285
from a backbone standpoint.

00:31:04.285 --> 00:31:06.930
So you can actually
have clusters

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:08.760
that are running in
different regions that

00:31:08.760 --> 00:31:12.990
are using the same VPC that can
actually talk to each other.

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:14.730
Pods can talk to each other.

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:16.650
That's a very cool
feature that's

00:31:16.650 --> 00:31:18.400
only available in Google Cloud.

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:20.400
BRETT HESTERBERG: Those
are great points, Allan.

