00:33:38.860 --> 00:33:41.380
And then we can take a quick
look at the YAML file, Allan,

00:33:41.380 --> 00:33:44.050
just to show folks what
what's inside of this one.

00:33:44.050 --> 00:33:46.270
ALLAN NALM: Yeah, so this
is a deployment file.

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:48.306
This is a Kubernetes
artifact, right.

00:33:48.306 --> 00:33:51.220
It's a specification that
you define that describes

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:52.870
your application.

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:56.590
And what we're doing
here is we are deploying

00:33:56.590 --> 00:34:01.180
a pod that contains the
[? Meme ?] Gen container, as

00:34:01.180 --> 00:34:05.500
well as the Cloud SQL proxy
as a sidecar container.

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:09.310
So a pod is really about
packaging containers

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:13.219
together that have very much
a dependency on each other,

00:34:13.219 --> 00:34:13.719
right?

00:34:13.719 --> 00:34:16.510
So any time you have
multiple containers

00:34:16.510 --> 00:34:19.600
that need to live
and die together,

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:23.650
or perhaps need to share
local IPC or share storage,

00:34:23.650 --> 00:34:24.790
you put those in a pod.

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:27.159
And it becomes a unit of scale.

00:34:27.159 --> 00:34:28.989
When you create a
pod in Kubernetes,

00:34:28.989 --> 00:34:31.760
every pod is assigned
an IP address.

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:34.360
So we have this
notion of IP per pod.

00:34:34.360 --> 00:34:38.530
And these are internal addresses
that are basically pulled out

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:40.430
of the CIDR block
ranges that are

00:34:40.430 --> 00:34:43.750
assigned for every node
within a Kubernetes cluster.

00:34:43.750 --> 00:34:47.710
By default, every node
gets a slash 24 CIDR range.

00:34:47.710 --> 00:34:50.380
And when a pod comes up
on that particular node,

00:34:50.380 --> 00:34:52.769
it takes an IP from that range.

00:34:52.769 --> 00:34:54.060
BRETT HESTERBERG: That's great.

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:57.030
So I just used our YAML
file to start the creation.

00:34:57.030 --> 00:34:58.990
And what we'll look
for is that our pods

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:01.110
