00:35:04.140 --> 00:35:09.000
So think of a deployment as a
Kubernetes controller, right?

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:11.190
So with the
deployment, Kubernetes

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:14.280
brings this notion of
a declarative model.

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:18.030
So within a deployment, you
specify your desired state,

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:21.490
what you'd like the world to
look like for your application,

00:35:21.490 --> 00:35:24.480
so how many instances.

00:35:24.480 --> 00:35:28.020
You can specify certain
labeling associated

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:29.490
with your application.

00:35:29.490 --> 00:35:34.020
A controller is constantly
monitoring the actual state

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:35.280
of your application.

00:35:35.280 --> 00:35:38.490
So if something bad
happens, a node goes down,

00:35:38.490 --> 00:35:42.240
a pod goes down, the controller
will look at the actual state

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:45.000
and then will compare
with the desired state

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:48.210
that you specify in
that deployment file,

00:35:48.210 --> 00:35:51.900
and will always ensure that
your actual state equals

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:53.010
your desired state.

00:35:53.010 --> 00:35:54.900
This is the beauty
in terms of being

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:57.180
able to deploy an
application that's

00:35:57.180 --> 00:35:59.130
highly available,
resilient, and not

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:03.200
have to worry
about ensuring that

00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:06.222
the application, the
number of instances.

00:36:06.222 --> 00:36:07.680
If something goes
wrong, Kubernetes

00:36:07.680 --> 00:36:10.830
is will always kick
in with health checks

00:36:10.830 --> 00:36:14.370
to ensure that your actual
state equals to that state

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:16.057
that your application needs.

00:36:16.057 --> 00:36:18.390
BRETT HESTERBERG: It's really
a notion of infrastructure

00:36:18.390 --> 00:36:20.040
by configuration
file in this case.

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:20.920
ALLAN NALM: Yep.

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:24.000
So what we're doing
now is we talked

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:27.600
about the whole notion of a pod
gets an internal IP address.

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:32.400
But let's say you want
that pod to be accessed

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:33.600
from the outside world.

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:37.050
And what Brett just did
is exposed the deployment

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:40.060
as a load balance service.

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:43.260
So what this actually does
it creates a service object,

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:47.700
and it creates a
GCE load balancer,

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:50.130
assigns it an
external IP address,

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:52.200
and creates the
routes associated

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:54.570
with that external
IP address back

00:36:54.570 --> 00:36:57.180
to the pods, the
internal pods, that

00:36:57.180 --> 00:36:59.100
are running in your cluster.

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:04.320
In our case here, we only
had one pod that was running.

00:37:04.320 --> 00:37:07.290
But think of a case where
you have 10 pods running

00:37:07.290 --> 00:37:09.240
across multiple machines.

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.280
Once you hit that
external IP, they'll

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:13.750
get round-robined to the
appropriate pods running

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:15.000
across your cluster.

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:18.690
So as you can see here, we
have an external IP address

00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:19.740
that's been assigned.

00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:21.450
And Brett can now hit it.

00:37:21.450 --> 00:37:25.060
And basically, it should
show the application.

00:37:25.060 --> 00:37:29.910
And for those of you
that have phones and want

00:37:29.910 --> 00:37:32.270
to hit that address,
please go in and meme.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:33.450
BRETT HESTERBERG: Yeah,
I think this is really

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:34.330
the moment of truth.

00:37:34.330 --> 00:37:35.955
We're going to find
out right now if we

00:37:35.955 --> 00:37:37.500
deployed this app publicly.

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:39.600
It looks like on our end
that this app is good.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:42.180
We're going to double
check our database here.

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:44.410
Looks like our lone
meme is still there.

00:37:44.410 --> 00:37:46.050
So as Allan
mentioned, if you have

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:48.480
mobile devices and a
pretty good set of eyes

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:49.810
here to read this URL--

00:37:49.810 --> 00:37:52.530
we can read it out to you.

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:54.225
Let me zoom this in, if we want.

00:37:54.225 --> 00:37:55.100
Here, let's just see.

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:00.090
I'm struggling to
zoom the IP address.

00:38:00.090 --> 00:38:02.020
I will read it quickly--

00:38:02.020 --> 00:38:06.150
35.226.10.149.

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:09.570
I suspect there are many
great memes in this audience,

00:38:09.570 --> 00:38:10.890
far better than mine.

00:38:10.890 --> 00:38:12.570
And to sweeten the
deal a bit, Allan

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:16.680
and I have come armed with $500
Google Cloud credit coupons.

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:18.300
We're happy to dole
those out to folks

00:38:18.300 --> 00:38:19.860
who create some
of the best memes

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:21.990
and hit us up after the event.

00:38:21.990 --> 00:38:24.030
One thing I will note
here is that this session

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:26.710
is being recorded and will
be broadcast on the internet.

00:38:26.710 --> 00:38:30.390
So let's please keep the
memes as tasteful as we can.

00:38:30.390 --> 00:38:33.360
But I look forward to seeing
what you come up with.

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:37.380
We talked to Allan at the
beginning about auto-scaling,

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:40.260
or about building a
scalable application.

00:38:40.260 --> 00:38:42.340
Let's start to
add an auto-scaler

00:38:42.340 --> 00:38:43.440
to our configuration.

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:45.648
And then we'll see if,
between the audience and maybe

00:38:45.648 --> 00:38:47.280
ourselves, we can
generate some load.

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:48.000
ALLAN NALM: Let's do that.

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:49.166
BRETT HESTERBERG: All right.

00:38:49.166 --> 00:38:52.180
And I'm looking forward,
by the way to-- whoa.

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:56.432
Some of the memes coming in.

00:38:56.432 --> 00:38:57.890
We're going to go
back to that tab.

00:38:57.890 --> 00:38:59.384
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:03.252
OK, so we just added
an auto-scaler.

00:39:03.252 --> 00:39:05.210
ALLAN NALM: Yeah, so what
we're doing right now

00:39:05.210 --> 00:39:10.540
is we're creating a horizontal
pod auto-scaler object.

00:39:10.540 --> 00:39:13.770
And what this does is it defines
a certain set of thresholds.

00:39:13.770 --> 00:39:15.300
So what we're
doing here is we're

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:19.380
saying, if the combined CPU
utilization across all my pods

00:39:19.380 --> 00:39:24.130
increases above 50%, I'd
like you to auto-scale.

00:39:24.130 --> 00:39:28.050
So if we take a look at
our HPA object right now,

00:39:28.050 --> 00:39:29.470
there's really no load.

00:39:29.470 --> 00:39:31.470
BRETT HESTERBERG: There's
no load at this point.

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:33.990
We should really see this
number at almost close to zero.

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:35.880
So we'll let this get
it initialized here.

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:37.050
And then, oh, it
looks like we've

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:39.174
got a little bit of load,
courtesy of the audience.

00:39:39.174 --> 00:39:40.410
Thank you.

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:42.870
Let's push this a bit.

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:48.720
Allan and I have set up a VM
that has an application called

00:39:48.720 --> 00:39:49.440
Hey installed.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:52.290
Hey is a web load generator.

00:39:52.290 --> 00:39:53.496
Let me bring this up.

00:39:53.496 --> 00:39:54.620
Bear with me just a moment.

00:39:54.620 --> 00:39:56.630
We'll add our load to the mix.

00:39:56.630 --> 00:39:58.610
And please keep
your memes coming.

00:39:58.610 --> 00:40:01.520
We'll see if we can knock
down our application here.

00:40:01.520 --> 00:40:02.520
Let's just take a look.

00:40:02.520 --> 00:40:05.924
I'm going to bring up
a terminal to this VM.

00:40:05.924 --> 00:40:07.340
And we'll get our
load kicked off.

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:09.390
And then we'll see how
Kubernetes handles things.

00:40:11.755 --> 00:40:13.380
And by the way, I'm
excited to get back

00:40:13.380 --> 00:40:16.619
to the tab to see what
you all are creating.

00:40:16.619 --> 00:40:18.660
ALLAN NALM: So it'll take
a minute or so for this

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:19.260
to come up.

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:24.330
And what'll actually happen is
the horizontal pod auto-scaler

00:40:24.330 --> 00:40:29.370
will be monitoring the
load usage coming in.

00:40:29.370 --> 00:40:31.890
And then as soon as
there's an average CPU

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:37.230
utilization, above 50%, you
should see a pod auto-scale.

00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:39.330
So we're talking about
auto-scaling here

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:40.560
at the pod level.

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:44.460
But keep in mind, we've also
enabled cluster auto-scaling

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:46.420
for the Kubernetes
Engine cluster.

00:40:46.420 --> 00:40:50.370
So if, for some reason, we
exceeded the resources that

00:40:50.370 --> 00:40:53.790
are available from
a node standpoint,

00:40:53.790 --> 00:40:58.710
Kubernetes Engine will actually
provision a new node, a new set

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:02.651
of nodes in our zones as well.

00:41:02.651 --> 00:41:04.650
BRETT HESTERBERG: OK, so
let's take a quick look

00:41:04.650 --> 00:41:06.450
and just see how
many pods we have.

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:08.850
We still have our
lone pod running.

00:41:08.850 --> 00:41:13.367
Hopefully, our load generator
has started to take effect.

00:41:13.367 --> 00:41:14.200
Let's just see here.

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:16.044
So we're getting close
to our target here.

00:41:16.044 --> 00:41:17.960
We'll see if between the
audience and our load

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:20.040
generator, we can
ratchet this up.

00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:24.300
What we're looking
for is a load number

00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:27.060
coming in that is in
excess of our 50% target.

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:30.510
When that happens, that tells
Kubernetes Engine, Allan,

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:32.730
to start to scale
by adding more pods,

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:35.562
adding more web front ends
behind our load balancer

00:41:35.562 --> 00:41:37.770
so that we can scale to meet
the load of our now very

00:41:37.770 --> 00:41:38.874
popular application.

00:41:38.874 --> 00:41:41.040
Let's take a quick look
here and see where we're at.

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:42.330
Still at 50.

00:41:42.330 --> 00:41:45.000
We'll see if we can get our
Load Gen tool spinning up

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:45.629
a bit more.

00:41:45.629 --> 00:41:47.670
ALLAN NALM: And these
thresholds-- you can also--

00:41:47.670 --> 00:41:49.060
here we're using CPU.

00:41:49.060 --> 00:41:50.990
You can also specify memory.

00:41:50.990 --> 00:41:53.295
And we also have
an option for you

00:41:53.295 --> 00:41:58.900
to define custom metrics,
so number of requests,

00:41:58.900 --> 00:42:00.870
queue length, things
of that nature,

00:42:00.870 --> 00:42:04.290
to trigger these horizontal
pod auto-scaling.

00:42:04.290 --> 00:42:07.470
So there we go, 266%.

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:10.620
So we've exceeded the
threshold, and Kubernetes now

00:42:10.620 --> 00:42:15.030
is auto-scaling the pods.

00:42:15.030 --> 00:42:16.780
And these pods are
being auto-scaled

00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:19.980
across the availability zones.

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:21.660
And there we go.

00:42:21.660 --> 00:42:25.290
So now we have four pods that
are running within our region.

00:42:25.290 --> 00:42:29.657
And this is the actual
state that the application

00:42:29.657 --> 00:42:30.240
is running in.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:32.367
And the controller
is monitoring us.

00:42:32.367 --> 00:42:34.950
BRETT HESTERBERG: It looks like
our app is still up and pretty

00:42:34.950 --> 00:42:36.330
healthy, even under this load.

00:42:36.330 --> 00:42:38.110
Let's give it a little
more to work with.

00:42:38.110 --> 00:42:40.910
I'm going to say that
something bad happens to,

00:42:40.910 --> 00:42:43.100
let's just say, our
original pod here.

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:45.160
ALLAN NALM: Yep, so
if we kill a pod,

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:48.660
the controller will check
to see that, hey, I've

00:42:48.660 --> 00:42:52.340
got only three pods
running, I should have four,

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:54.750
and will automatically
create a new pod

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:58.590
so that you constantly
have enough capacity

00:42:58.590 --> 00:42:59.792
to serve your application.

00:42:59.792 --> 00:43:02.250
BRETT HESTERBERG: All right,
it looks like we fired up one,

00:43:02.250 --> 00:43:03.000
and it's a replacement.

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:05.070
I'm going to see if I can
give it a little more work.

00:43:05.070 --> 00:43:07.111
We'll kill one more pod
just to see what happens.

00:43:07.111 --> 00:43:10.680
And while we kill that, let's
go take a look at our web app.

00:43:10.680 --> 00:43:13.360
It looks like our web app is
still living pretty healthy.

00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:13.860
Wow.

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:17.894
We've got-- did
someone script this?

00:43:17.894 --> 00:43:19.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:23.294
That may be worth a
coupon in and of itself.

00:43:23.294 --> 00:43:25.460
Looks like our web front
ends are handling this load

00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:26.040
pretty well.

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:29.980
Let's imagine now that we have
a problem on the database side.

00:43:29.980 --> 00:43:33.040
So I'm going to go over
to our Cloud SQL instance.

00:43:33.040 --> 00:43:35.310
And let's give it a problem.

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:39.750
I'll start by noting that our
Cloud SQL instance is happily

00:43:39.750 --> 00:43:44.390
existing in Zone B of US
central 1, Zone Bravo.

00:43:44.390 --> 00:43:50.860
Let's ask Cloud SQL to
failover this instance.

00:43:50.860 --> 00:43:53.415
I'm going to initiate
a failover here.

00:43:57.620 --> 00:43:58.710
We'll start this.

00:43:58.710 --> 00:44:00.840
And Cloud SQL says this
operation is starting.

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:03.390
So what we've done here
is simulate a failure.

00:44:03.390 --> 00:44:07.350
Typically, Cloud SQL will be
health checking our instance.

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:10.590
And if for some reason the
instance becomes unhealthy,

00:44:10.590 --> 00:44:13.320
Cloud SQL will trigger this
failover automatically.

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:15.542
Here, because we
just want to show

00:44:15.542 --> 00:44:17.250
what this looks like
for our application,

00:44:17.250 --> 00:44:19.980
we're testing a failover
by manually triggering it.

00:44:19.980 --> 00:44:23.320
So Cloud SQL thinks the failover
operation's in progress.

00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:25.560
When this happens,
our application

00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:28.680
attempts to connect to
the database and it fails.

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:30.720
So what's going on
right now is Cloud SQL

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:33.020
had previously provisioned
our primary instance

00:44:33.020 --> 00:44:36.450
in Zone B. It provisioned
a standby instance

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:38.120
in some other zone
within the region.

00:44:38.120 --> 00:44:40.036
It always makes sure
it's in a different zone,

00:44:40.036 --> 00:44:41.190
a different failure domain.

00:44:41.190 --> 00:44:43.809
Cloud SQL is making sure
that our primary instance,

00:44:43.809 --> 00:44:46.350
our old primary instance, has
come down, won't serve any more

00:44:46.350 --> 00:44:47.760
write requests.

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:50.970
And then it's bringing
up our standby instance.

00:44:50.970 --> 00:44:54.000
After doing so, it's going
to move the name and the IP

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:58.210
address of our primary instance
over to the standby instance,

00:44:58.210 --> 00:45:00.820
making it primary as well.

00:45:00.820 --> 00:45:03.810
This is important because
to your application, when

00:45:03.810 --> 00:45:06.000
a failover occurs,
it looks simply

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:09.470
like a database goes down
and then comes back up.

00:45:09.470 --> 00:45:12.150
There's no need to
build in failover logic

00:45:12.150 --> 00:45:13.440
to the application.

00:45:13.440 --> 00:45:16.730
Cloud SQL is orchestrating
those mechanics itself.

00:45:16.730 --> 00:45:20.190
After it's bringing up our
new primary instance to life,

00:45:20.190 --> 00:45:21.750
Cloud SQL's going
off and building

00:45:21.750 --> 00:45:24.270
another standby instance
in yet another zone

00:45:24.270 --> 00:45:27.300
to make sure that our new
primary instance is protected.

00:45:27.300 --> 00:45:29.130
Usually this whole
failover scenario

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:32.350
with Cloud SQL for Postgres
takes about 45 seconds.

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:36.360
So let's check back in and
see what Cloud SQL has to say.

00:45:36.360 --> 00:45:38.950
Cloud SQL looks like
it's healthy again.

00:45:38.950 --> 00:45:41.850
And we'll try to note
now, our primary instance

00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:45.330
is no longer in Zone B. We're
now in Zone F, as in Foxtrot.

00:45:45.330 --> 00:45:47.790
So we failed from
Zone B to Zone F.

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:51.350
Let's see if our
application is happy again.

00:45:51.350 --> 00:45:53.610
With any luck, we'll
do a refresh here.

00:45:53.610 --> 00:45:55.190
And it looks like we're back.

00:45:55.190 --> 00:45:57.810
Ooh, wow.

00:45:57.810 --> 00:45:59.340
Well done.

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:02.610
So what we've seen
here, Allan, is

00:46:02.610 --> 00:46:05.272
that we've injected some
faults in our application.

00:46:05.272 --> 00:46:07.230
And it looks like we've
successfully recovered.

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:08.940
We've coped with the
load from our VM,

00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:11.550
as well as from
our audience here.

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:15.060
With that, let me switch
back now to our slides.

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:16.634
And we'll do a little
bit of wrap up.

00:46:16.634 --> 00:46:18.300
So again, I think a
couple of key points

00:46:18.300 --> 00:46:21.210
in our architecture here-- you
saw the auto-scaling nature

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:22.590
of Kubernetes Engine.

00:46:22.590 --> 00:46:25.320
As we increased load,
Kubernetes automatically

00:46:25.320 --> 00:46:27.150
added pods on our behalf.

00:46:27.150 --> 00:46:29.830
You also saw how
Kubernetes Engine and Cloud

00:46:29.830 --> 00:46:31.590
SQL handle failures.

00:46:31.590 --> 00:46:34.050
Our application was down
for about 45 seconds

00:46:34.050 --> 00:46:36.090
in total, while Cloud
SQL was failing over.

00:46:36.090 --> 00:46:39.060
It wasn't down at all when
Kubernetes pods failed.

00:46:39.060 --> 00:46:41.970
The upshot for Allan
and I is that we didn't

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:43.950
get a text or email about this.

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:46.440
This happened without us
taking any intervention.

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:48.552
And you all are happily
creating memes again.

00:46:48.552 --> 00:46:50.760
So this, I think, really
speaks to the fault tolerant

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:53.460
nature of this architecture.

00:46:53.460 --> 00:46:57.180
We want to wrap up today
with just a few next steps.

00:46:57.180 --> 00:46:59.910
As a starting point, please
try this out yourself

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:02.260
with the code lab
that's available online.

